A format list where a single-channel format disagrees with a multi-channel one: keep the tiling

Relations: requires patches/0005-turnip-mutable-uniform-swap-tiling.patch -
it adds tu6_format_list_swaps_are_uniform(), and this patch extends
its rule. Without 0005 it does not apply (context in tu_formats.cc)
and is semantically meaningless.

What is wrong. The function requires the swap of every format in the
list to match, and the single R32_UINT in the list is to blame:
B8G8R8A8_UNORM carries WXYZ while R32_* carries WZYX. But on tiling,
no swap for the view exists at all. fd6_pipe2swap() returns WZYX to
every format except is_mutable (fd6_format_table.c:396), so on tiling
the difference between WXYZ and WZYX has no representation and cannot
be the cause of demotion to linear. The linear path, for its part,
comes from the same tu6_format_texture(..., TILE6_LINEAR, false).

What changes. The single-channel format is excluded from the
channel-order comparison: it has no channel order, there is nothing to
argue about. The block-shape comparison (bits, width, height) stays for
everyone, because the tile layout depends only on the block size
(fd6_layout.c:115). The remaining checks - channel count, swizzle,
channel sizes - are kept unchanged, and the reference is now taken from
the first format in the list that has a channel order at all.

Why. This is exactly the list vkd3d-proton builds for typeless-BGRA8
with D3D12_RESOURCE_FLAG_ALLOW_UNORDERED_ACCESS: the D3D11 typed
UAV-load rule (resource.c:393-401) appends R32{U,I,F} to every 32-bit
typeless format. Out of the 72 classes of tests/vkd3d-fmt-classes.h,
exactly two go to linear on stock - B8G8R8A8_TYPELESS+UAV and
B8G8R8X8_TYPELESS+UAV, i.e. vkd3d's most common list for a 32-bit
texture. With this patch no vkd3d class on gen2 goes to linear.

What is untouched. The cross-order {BGRA8, RGBA8} - two multi-channel
formats with opposite channel order - and NV12 stay linear: there the
disagreement is real. Verified by enumeration
(scripts/fmt-list-gate-check.py --exp-0009) and on hardware.

What it costs, measured on the 740. The BGRA8 side - fill and readback
with the image format - is untouched, roundtrip EXACT 0/262144. What
changes is visibility through a second view: the R32_UINT view returns
a word with bytes 0 and 2 swapped, 261120/262144 words diverge from
the linear path. Under D3D12 this is safe: a typed UAV load of BGRA8
returns float4 and is read through a view of the resource's own
format, and a resource declared ALLOW_UNORDERED_ACCESS stays typed
BGRA8, so a raw 32-bit read through an R32 view is inexpressible in
D3D12 anyway. Analysis, logs and probe - docs/analysis.md, section 21.

diff --git a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc
--- a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc
+++ b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc
@@ -170,6 +170,25 @@
  * swap, which R8G8B8A8 and B8G8R8A8 share.  Those two are not
  * reinterpretations of each other, a list mixing them still has to fall back
  * to linear.
+ *
+ * A format with a single channel has no channel order for two views to
+ * disagree about: R32_UINT reads the stored word as it is, however that word
+ * was written.  Such a format must not by itself push the list to linear; it
+ * only has to agree on the texel block, because the tiled layout is a
+ * function of the block size alone (fd6_layout.c: layout->cpp =
+ * util_format_get_blocksize()).
+ *
+ * The list this buys is the one vkd3d builds for a typeless BGRA8 resource
+ * created with D3D12_RESOURCE_FLAG_ALLOW_UNORDERED_ACCESS: the D3D11 typed
+ * UAV load rule (vkd3d-proton resource.c:393-401) appends R32{U,I,F} to every
+ * 32-bit typeless format.  On the stock check that list went linear, because
+ * B8G8R8A8 carries the WXYZ swap and R32 does not - but on the tiled layout
+ * fd6_pipe2swap() hands WZYX to *every* format that is not is_mutable
+ * (fd6_format_table.c:396), so that difference has no representation there
+ * and cannot be what demotes the image.
+ *
+ * The lists that must keep going to linear are unaffected: {BGRA8, RGBA8}
+ * (two multi-channel formats, opposite channel order) and NV12.
  */
 bool
 tu6_format_list_swaps_are_uniform(const VkImageFormatListCreateInfo *fmt_list)
@@ -177,18 +196,12 @@
    if (!fmt_list || fmt_list->viewFormatCount < 2)
       return false;
 
-   VkFormat first_vk_format = fmt_list->pViewFormats[0];
-   const struct util_format_description *first =
-      util_format_description(vk_format_to_pipe_format(first_vk_format));
-   enum a3xx_color_swap swap =
-      tu6_format_texture(vk_format_to_pipe_format(first_vk_format),
-                         TILE6_LINEAR, false)
-         .swap;
-
-   for (uint32_t i = 1; i < fmt_list->viewFormatCount; i++) {
+   /* The reference for everything about channel order is the first format
+    * in the list that has a channel order at all.
+    */
+   enum pipe_format ref_format = PIPE_FORMAT_NONE;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < fmt_list->viewFormatCount; i++) {
       VkFormat vk_format = fmt_list->pViewFormats[i];
-      const struct util_format_description *desc =
-         util_format_description(vk_format_to_pipe_format(vk_format));
 
       /* Layouts we don't model here (depth/stencil, compressed, multi-planar)
        * are not something a description comparison can speak about.
@@ -198,13 +211,40 @@
           vk_format_get_plane_count(vk_format) > 1)
          return false;
 
-      if (desc->nr_channels != first->nr_channels ||
-          desc->block.bits != first->block.bits ||
-          desc->block.width != first->block.width ||
-          desc->block.height != first->block.height ||
-          tu6_format_texture(vk_format_to_pipe_format(vk_format),
-                             TILE6_LINEAR, false)
-                  .swap != swap)
+      if (util_format_description(vk_format_to_pipe_format(vk_format))
+              ->nr_channels > 1) {
+         ref_format = vk_format_to_pipe_format(vk_format);
+         break;
+      }
+   }
+
+   /* Nothing in the list has more than one channel, so nothing in it can
+    * disagree about the order of the channels of a stored word.
+    */
+   if (ref_format == PIPE_FORMAT_NONE)
+      return true;
+
+   const struct util_format_description *ref =
+      util_format_description(ref_format);
+   enum a3xx_color_swap swap =
+      tu6_format_texture(ref_format, TILE6_LINEAR, false).swap;
+
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < fmt_list->viewFormatCount; i++) {
+      enum pipe_format format = vk_format_to_pipe_format(fmt_list->pViewFormats[i]);
+      const struct util_format_description *desc =
+         util_format_description(format);
+
+      if (desc->block.bits != ref->block.bits ||
+          desc->block.width != ref->block.width ||
+          desc->block.height != ref->block.height)
+         return false;
+
+      /* A single channel has no channel order to disagree about. */
+      if (desc->nr_channels < 2)
+         continue;
+
+      if (desc->nr_channels != ref->nr_channels ||
+          tu6_format_texture(format, TILE6_LINEAR, false).swap != swap)
          return false;
 
       /* The swizzle is what says which channel of the stored word ends up
@@ -212,8 +252,8 @@
        * apart.
        */
       for (unsigned c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
-         if (desc->swizzle[c] != first->swizzle[c] ||
-             desc->channel[c].size != first->channel[c].size)
+         if (desc->swizzle[c] != ref->swizzle[c] ||
+             desc->channel[c].size != ref->channel[c].size)
             return false;
       }
    }
